  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 241 PM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nThe pattern this week will be driven by a persistent high\npressure over Ontario and Quebec with multiple shortwaves aloft\nover the Dakotas. This will provide for rain chances Wednesday\nnight into Thursday and Saturday into Saturday night. Due to \nthis setup rain chances and QPF is maximized across the Dakotas \ninto western Minnesota and falls off quickly across eastern \nMinnesota and Wisconsin as the high helps bring in dry air.\n\nToday and Tomorrow... Although we remain in a ridge right now,\nthere is a small are of vorticity across Iowa whose impact is \nshown in both radar and satellite with increased clouds and some\nisolated rain showers. We have no chance for rain up here, but \nsome of the cloud cover associated with this has covered much of\nsouthern Minnesota today. Farther north we have some some \ndiurnal cumulus as well. This evening the feature over Iowa will\nmove out and the diurnal cumulus will dissipate providing for a\nmostly clear night. Temperatures will again fall and some \nisolated areas of frost cannot be ruled out in northwest \nWisconsin. It does not look widespread enough however, for a \nfrost advisory. Tomorrow will be fairly similar to today with \nhighs in the mid 60s and mostly clear skies. Cloud cover will \nincrease from west to east and our next system approaches.\n\nWednesday night and Thursday... With an approaching weak jet\nstreak, 500 mb shortwave, and some 850 mb WAA multiple levels \nof synoptic lift will be present Wednesday night into Thursday. \nPrecipitable water values look high enough as well to support \nrain across western Minnesota. Dry air across eastern \nMinnesota/western Wisconsin combined with less synoptic forcing \nhave QPF values focused on western Minnesota across ensemble \nmembers and the NBM. This doesn't mean no rain across eastern\nMinnesota and into Wisconsin just less rain. SPC shows a day 2 \ngeneral thunder risk, but not a day 3. So that initial wave \ncould have enough instability for some thunder, but overall \nlooks more like rain showers.\n\nFriday and Saturday...Friday a system looks to be moving across\nthe central plains and the northern edge of this could provide \nfor some rain across southwestern Minnesota. For the rest of us \nthe impact will be more cloud cover rather than a good chance \nfor rain. Some warm air advection looks to be present across the\nregion Friday so despite the clouds, temperatures could be \nslightly warmer in the mid to upper 60s for highs. Saturday sees\na setup very similar to Wednesday night into thursday with a \nshortwave over the Dakotas and the persistent high still to the \neast. So once again the best rain chances across western \nMinnesota falling off the the east.\n\nEarly Next Week... The pattern does look to shift early next \nweek with a larger system looking to move into the Midwest, the \ntrack of this low is still highly variable so confidence in \nwhere the rain would fall is still high. Temperatures before \nthis shift in the pattern, so through Saturday, are forecast to\nbe fairly steady in the 60s (favoring lower to mid 60s). Early\nnext week this looks to shift more into above normal territory\nin the upper 60s to lower 70s. Normals this time of year are \nright around 70 today falling into the upper 60s this weekend. \nSo the shift to above normal next week has about as much to do \nwith falling normals as it does some slightly warmer highs. We\nare now in the time of year with some of the fastest decreases\nin temperature normals.",
